# Why PHP Variables Disappear After jQuery Clicks

The core issue preventing your `$var1` from being accessible lies in how web servers process requests versus how JavaScript executes. When you click a button and run PHP code directly inside a jQuery event handler, that code runs on the client side (in the browser), not on the server. Because of this fundamental separation, attempting to assign a value to a PHP variable within a JavaScript function is impossible for subsequent server-side logic to read.

The snippet `<?php $var1="1"; ?>` inside your `.click()` handler executes immediately when the page loads or as soon as the browser parses the script tag. However, because it is embedded within a JavaScript context, the PHP parser never sees it as a server-side instruction. Instead, the browser's JavaScript engine interprets `<?php` as literal text characters. Consequently, when you later check `if(isset($var1))`, the variable `$var1` has never been initialized on the server during that specific request cycle; it simply does not exist in the current scope of the PHP execution flow.

To pass a value from a jQuery event to a PHP variable without submitting a form, you must use an asynchronous AJAX request. This approach sends data from the client back to the server, triggering a fresh PHP execution context where the variable can be properly stored and retrieved.

Here is how you would implement this using vanilla JavaScript or jQuery's `$.ajax` method:

```javascript
$(".next").click(function() {
    $.ajax({
        url: '/update-value', // Replace with your actual endpoint
        type: 'POST',
        data: {
            var1: '1'
        },
        success: function(response) {
            console.log('Server received the value:', response);
            // Optional: Refresh the page or update the DOM if needed
            location.reload(); 
        }
    });
});
```

In this example, clicking the button triggers a POST request to a specific URL. The server receives the data in `$_POST['var1']`. You can then assign this value to `$var1` in your controller or route handler. Once the server processes the request and returns a response (or redirects), the new PHP execution context will contain the variable, allowing your `isset()` check to succeed on a subsequent load.

If you are working within the Laravel ecosystem, there is a more elegant pattern that avoids manual AJAX implementation for simple state updates. You can leverage the **Flash Data** feature or session storage if the user remains on the same page after an action. For instance, you could configure a route to handle the button click and store the value in `session()`, then retrieve it in the next request via `$request->session()->get('var1')`.

Alternatively, if you need the variable available immediately without reloading the page, consider using JavaScript's native `localStorage` or `sessionStorage`. While this stores data on the client side rather than the server, it effectively bypasses the server-side execution gap. You can write to storage in jQuery and read it back in a new PHP view by passing the value via URL parameters or reconstructing the state based on the stored key.

> If you are looking for robust ways to handle state management between views without complex AJAX setups, check out the solutions provided at [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com). They offer insights into leveraging Laravel's session and flash data mechanisms effectively.

The trade-off here is architectural: direct assignment in a click handler works only if you accept that the code runs on the client (requiring storage like localStorage) or if you are willing to trigger a server request (AJAX). Trying to mix PHP syntax directly inside JavaScript event listeners without an actual server round-trip will always result in the variable being undefined on the server side.
